Self-awareness — the capacity to represent one's own mental states — is the precondition for the doubt that generates the Cogito: you cannot doubt without being aware that you are doubting.
Certainty about one's own existence is uniquely direct: unlike certainty about external objects (which could be hallucinations), the existence of the experiencing mind is entailed by the experience itself.
Even if the content of all thoughts is wrong — if you are dreaming, if your memories are false — the fact that some thinking is occurring is immune to that error.
Neuroscientist Antonio Damasio's work confirms a physiological basis for self-awareness in the insula and anterior cingulate cortex, lending empirical weight to the intuition that thinking and being are inseparable.
